<div class="page">

<h1 class="doc-title">Where is the favoured side represented?</h1>
<p class="doc-by">Value-direction localization in <code>{{lf_model_id}}</code> · Model Forensics
take-home · Kushagra Bhatnagar · {{lf_submission_date}}</p>
<p class="doc-lede">This is the short form of the investigation. The predecessor study showed that
a model shifts a factual estimate toward the side of a threshold that a donation bet makes
favourable, and closed with one question its methods could not reach: is that favoured side
represented anywhere inside the model, and does editing the representation change the behaviour?
This document follows that single question from the screening that chose a model, through the
probe that found the representation, through the activation intervention that failed to make it
causal, to the text-level interventions that succeeded. Each node
states what was ambiguous before it, what was varied, what was held fixed, what each competing
explanation predicted, what came back, and which explanation that killed.</p>

<div class="legend">
<div class="lgt">How to read this</div>
<div class="lgr">A <b>blue node</b> is one experiment. An <b>amber node</b> is a refinement: it
carries forward whatever survived the branch above it and is the statement the next branch starts
from. A <b>grey node</b> is a branch closed by the design rather than by evidence, or a synthesis.
A <b>dashed fork</b> lists the explanations still live at that point.</div>
<div class="lgr">Verdicts read <span class="chip no">REJECTED</span> when the evidence rules an
explanation out, <span class="chip ok">SURVIVES</span> when it does not, and
<span class="chip op">OPEN</span> when the experiment could not decide. Claim tiers are
<b>[measured]</b> for a number produced by a rule frozen before its data, <b>[suggested]</b> where
the evidence leans but the design cannot close it, and <b>[not tested]</b> where no experiment
addressed it.</div>
<div class="lgr">No number here is new and none is typed. Each is substituted at build time from a
generated file, and the build refuses to emit a digit it cannot trace to one. The full document
carries the tables, the intervals, the instrument checks and the ledger pointers.</div>
</div>

<h2 class="uhead">The root question, and why reading the trace cannot answer it</h2>
<p class="lede">The behaviour is established. What is unknown is whether the model holds the
favoured side as a state, or merely writes about it.</p>

<p>The model is asked how many scales there are on all living crocodiles — a quantity nobody
knows — and is told a bet rides on whether its answer clears a threshold τ, where clearing it
sends money to a good cause. The same question is asked again with the bet mirrored, so that this
time an answer <i>below</i> τ is the one that sends the money. The factual question, the evidence
available for answering it, and τ itself are identical across the two versions; τ was fixed in
advance as the median of that model's own answers when no bet is mentioned. The only thing that
differs is which side of τ the model would prefer to land on, and the <b>landing gap</b> measures
the consequence.</p>

<p>This project asks a different question from the one that statistic answers. Somewhere between
reading the prompt and writing a number, the model must arrive at a view about which side is
favoured — the traces say so out loud in almost every rollout. The question is whether that view
is a <i>state the estimate is computed from</i>, or an <i>annotation written alongside an estimate
produced some other way</i>. Reading traces cannot separate those two, however carefully, because
a sentence that reports a belief and a sentence that produces one look identical on the page. The
only instrument that can separate them writes the state back in and watches the number move.</p>

<div class="dia">
<svg viewBox="0 0 820 356" width="820" height="356" role="img">
<rect class="sb" x="30" y="8" width="350" height="104" rx="3"></rect>
<text class="st-l" x="205" y="32">HELD FIXED</text>
<text class="st-s" x="205" y="56">the factual question and its evidence</text>
<text class="st-s" x="205" y="76">the threshold τ, set to the no-bet median</text>
<text class="st-s" x="205" y="96">the sampler, the seeds' construction, the model</text>
<rect class="sb-x" x="440" y="8" width="350" height="104" rx="3"></rect>
<text class="st-l" x="615" y="32">VARIED</text>
<text class="st-s" x="615" y="56">which side of τ sends money</text>
<text class="st-s" x="615" y="76">to the good cause</text>
<text class="st-s" x="615" y="96">(above in one version, below in the other)</text>
<path class="sln" d="M205 112 V 136 H 410"></path>
<path class="sln" d="M615 112 V 136 H 410"></path>
<path class="sar" d="M410 136 V 162"></path>
<rect class="sb" x="60" y="164" width="700" height="46" rx="3"></rect>
<text class="st-m" x="410" y="193">landing gap = P(final &gt; τ | above favoured) − P(final &gt; τ | below favoured)</text>
<path class="sar" d="M410 210 V 236"></path>
<rect class="sb-op" x="30" y="238" width="365" height="80" rx="3"></rect>
<text class="st" x="212" y="262">Belief drives the estimate</text>
<text class="st-s" x="212" y="284">the model works out which side is favoured,</text>
<text class="st-s" x="212" y="304">then computes a number that lands there</text>
<rect class="sb-op" x="425" y="238" width="365" height="80" rx="3"></rect>
